{"i":829,"role":"assistant","text":"It's now called a **C / C++ Script**, and you can make C scripts too, choosing the language when you create one. I'd called them \"behaviours\" only to tell them apart from Lua scripts, but \"script\" matches Lua and how Unreal users talk, so I renamed it everywhere you see it.\n\nYour message was cut off at \"switch between the\". I took it as choosing **C++ or C** when creating a script. If you meant something else, tell me.\n\n**What changed:**\n- **Tools > New C / C++ Script…** has a **C++ | C** switch. C++ writes `Source/<Name>.cpp` and C writes `Source/<Name>.c`, then it compiles.\n- C and C++ scripts can sit side by side in `Source/`. A C script registers itself with one line at the end (`LSD_C_SCRIPT(...)`), so you never list scripts anywhere.\n- The **Native Script** component's field is now **Script**, and the property section is **Script properties**.\n- Console commands are now `game.NewScript <Name>` (add `c` for C) and `game.Scripts`.\n\n**Tested:**\n- In the real editor, a new C script compiled with MSVC, loaded and spun its cube in Play.\n- In the same build, your **Spinner.cpp** from the old template still compiled and loaded next to it, so old and new files work together.\n- A unit test with a real `.c` file in the test program checks that C scripts register themselves and run.\n\nI didn't run a new C++ script end to end this time (the test's first command arrived before the editor was listening), but that path passed earlier and its code didn't change, and the unit test still exercises it.\n\nDebug and Release build with 0 warnings and all 14 test suites pass. I committed and pushed my changes as `1bd0973`, and updated the docs: [CODING.md](docs/CODING.md) now shows a whole C script, and [How to code](docs/How%20to%20code%20in%20LSD_Engine.md) section 11 explains the C++ | C choice.\n\n**Left alone:** your `Source/` folder (Spinner) and your `NewLevel` edits aren't committed. I copied and restored them byte-for-byte around my test. Say if you want them committed."}
